      "location": "Proof step 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 says: \"By [L1], the machine code e primitive-recursively yields the finite transition table of M.\" But [L1] cites effective decoding. The cited definition expressly limits that notion: \"Effectiveness here means injective plus explicitly decodable with a fixed malformed-code output. It does not yet formalize computation by Turing machines.\" Effective decodability therefore does not establish a primitive-recursive decoder or transition-table extractor. The required bounded-arithmetic construction is not supplied or cited.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3a79b774e24e70092d0ce7ab67873ff78cdf4256192472d4ef6df574408a82ce"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:10:3",
      "id": "thm-multitape-to-single-tape-time-simulation",
      "location": "Proof step 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The cited quadratic-overhead lemma gives only \"time_{S_M}(w)≤c_M(|w|+t+1)^2.\" Step 2.1 then concludes O(T(n)^2) from t≤Time_M(n)=O(T(n)), but neither the theorem nor its assumptions gives n=O(T(n)). For example, a multitape machine that accepts every input after one transition has Time_M(n)=O(1), while the cited standard simulator initializes by making sweeps and its \"first block initially records the input,\" taking Ω(n) time. Thus the quoted bound cannot yield the asserted conclusion for the selected simulator without an input-reading lower-bound hypothesis or a different simulation construction.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "bcdad272cef6b2e1242dbf7dbf691dcd6d63b2ab19ff37de60eb485e366dfff1"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:10:4",
      "id": "thm-multitape-to-single-tape-space-simulation",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 says the standard simulator's blocks \"contain exactly those visited symbols.\" The cited standard-simulation construction instead says: \"The first block initially records the input.\" A machine that accepts after one transition visits only O(1) cells, but on an n-symbol input that standard simulator stores an n-symbol first block. Hence the asserted description of the simulator, and the resulting O(S(n)) inference for that simulator when S(n)=1, are false.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "07b267a1ef1e23927adea6af04111ac45b0491c1cc1cea8eb31b5685283e213f"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:10:5",
      "id": "prop-polynomially-related-encodings-preserve-polynomial-classes",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The statement assumes polynomial translators only on the valid-code domains σ[I] and τ[I]. Its proof therefore establishes behavior only on valid codes, yet DTIME/DSPACE are classes of languages decided by machines on all binary inputs. The cited encoding definition explicitly says that its total decoder is merely specified and that effectiveness \"does not yet formalize computation by Turing machines,\" so it supplies no procedure for recognizing malformed codes and rejecting them. The result needs decidable valid-code images, total polynomial translators with specified malformed-code behavior, or an explicit promise-problem convention; without one, the composed machine need not be a decider for the encoded language.",
      "severity": "fatal",
